  "query_preview": "..\nWednesday through Friday: Southwest flow aloft will persist across \nthe Inland Northwest on Wednesday between a digging Gulf of Alaska \nlow to the northwest and an amplified Four Corners ridge over the \ncentral US. An embedded shortwave will move through the region on \nWednesday, though models are showing the best instability across \nsoutheast Idaho. Because of this, no thunderstorms are expected with \nthis wave, though temperatures will remain near seasonal averages \nwith heights in the mid 80s to low 90s. On Thursday and Friday, the \nFour Corners ridge amplifies into the Inland Northwest as the Gulf \nof Alaska low nears the Pacific Northwest coast, initiating a brief \nwarming trend. 850mb temperatures warm to 22-24C across the Inland \nNorthwest by Friday, supporting highs in the low to mid 90s. Not the \nwarmest temperatures we have seen so far this year, but warm enough \nto bring localized Moderate (Orange) HeatRisk to urban areas and \nlower elevation river valleys. \n\n\u2026WIDESPREAD CRITICAL FIRE WEATHER CONDITIONS ON SATURDAY\u2026\n\nSaturday and Sunday: Ensemble guidance is in good agreement that the \nGulf of Alaska low will move inland on Saturday, driving a dynamic \ncold front across the Inland Northwest Saturday afternoon. While \nthis front will not be completely dry as it moves inland, with \nappreciable precipitation forecasted for the west slopes of the \nCascades. Unfortunately outside of the Cascade crest, all of the \nassociated moisture will be wrung out of the front as it crosses the \nmountains, moving through the Inland Northwest as a dry cold front. \nThis system features a powerful upper-level jet streak (up to 120 \nmph) shifting into the Pacific Northwest and leeside cyclogenesis \nover southern Alberta deepening a surface low to 994-1000 mb. This \nsetup drives a strong regional pressure gradient (YKM-YQL climbing \nto +10 to +15 mb), supporting widespread gusty winds. NBM \nprobabilities indicate an 80-100% chance for wind gusts of 40 mph \nand greater across the Inland Northwest. The strongest winds will be \nfelt across the Waterville Plateau and the western Columbia Basin \nwhere there is a 50-80% chance for wind gusts greater than 45 mph. \nAdditionally as the front moves through, ensembles are showing \nprecipitable water values dropping from 0.70-0.90 inches ahead of \nthe front to 0.35-0.50 inches behind the front, with minimum \nrelative humidity values in the 15 to 25 percent range Saturday \nafternoon. The combination of these strong winds and low relative \nhumidity values following a hot day will result in critical fire \nweather conditions across much of the Inland Northwest Saturday \nafternoon and evening. Any new or ongoing fires will spread rapidly. \n Weak post-frontal instability near the Cascade crest may support a \nslight chance (10-15%) of lightning along the immediate crest, \nthough confidence in thunderstorms stretching east of the crest is \nlow.\n\nAnother shortwave slides southeastward along the BC coast into the \nPNW on Sunday with breezy winds, especially through the Cascade gaps \ninto the western Columbia Basin, continuing on Sunday.  While dry \nconditions and elevated fire weather risks persist, ensembles \nindicate strong agreement for a noticeable cooling trend, with highs \ndropping into the mid 70s to low 80s.\n\nMonday and Tuesday: Ensembles generally favor upper-level ridging \nrebuilding over the Pacific Northwest early next week, initiating \nanother warming trend. Uncertainty remains regarding the strength \nand duration of the ridge, with 11% of ensembles suggesting the next \nweather system moving into the region by mid-week. /vmt",
